1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,513
Same code. Same pom. You build twice. And you get two different jars. Why?

2
00:00:06,063 --> 00:00:09,212
Because a build has loose ends: dates inside the jar,

3
00:00:09,212 --> 00:00:11,172
settings that differ per machine,

4
00:00:11,172 --> 00:00:12,954
and tools that change version.

5
00:00:13,504 --> 00:00:18,731
Today you tighten them. Plug ins, properties, profiles, and reproducible builds.

6
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:22,395
You will see each one live, then fix a project yourself.

7
00:00:22,945 --> 00:00:24,017
One honest note.

8
00:00:24,017 --> 00:00:27,301
My recording computer cannot reach Maven Central,

9
00:00:27,301 --> 00:00:30,383
so the demos use a local copy of the plug ins.

10
00:00:30,383 --> 00:00:33,399
The commands are the same ones you will type.

11
00:00:34,949 --> 00:00:38,234
Maven itself does almost nothing. Plug ins do the work.

12
00:00:38,784 --> 00:00:43,712
Every phase you saw last time runs a plug in goal. Compiler, sure fire, jar.

13
00:00:44,262 --> 00:00:47,161
You tune a plug in with a configuration block,

14
00:00:47,161 --> 00:00:48,358
inside the plug in.

15
00:00:48,358 --> 00:00:49,114
For example,

16
00:00:49,114 --> 00:00:52,390
tell the jar plug in which class starts the program.

17
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:58,316
That goes in the manifest, as the main class. Then java dash jar knows where to begin.

18
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:03,922
And pin every plug in version. A plug in that updates by itself can change your build.

19
00:01:05,972 --> 00:01:09,151
Properties. A value, defined once, used many times.

20
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:11,970
Write it inside the properties block.

21
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:13,994
Then use it anywhere with dollar,

22
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:14,791
curly braces,

23
00:01:14,791 --> 00:01:15,589
and its name.

24
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:20,917
Maven has its own too. Project dot version is the version of your project.

25
00:01:21,467 --> 00:01:26,566
Resource filtering copies a file into the jar, and fills in the dollar names on the way.

26
00:01:27,116 --> 00:01:32,087
And a value given on the command line, with dash capital D, always beats the pom.

27
00:01:33,637 --> 00:01:37,584
Profiles. A named set of changes, switched on only when you ask.

28
00:01:38,134 --> 00:01:41,568
Switch one on with dash capital P, and the profile name.

29
00:01:42,118 --> 00:01:47,260
Use profiles for differences, like dev and prod. Keep them few, and keep them small.

30
00:01:47,810 --> 00:01:50,568
Never put passwords in a profile in the pom.

31
00:01:50,568 --> 00:01:51,697
The pom is shared.

32
00:01:51,697 --> 00:01:53,954
We handle secrets in a later lesson.

33
00:01:56,004 --> 00:01:59,161
Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

34
00:01:59,711 --> 00:02:05,322
Read the settings. Type: sed -n 8,19p pom.xml

35
00:02:05,452 --> 00:02:06,393
Two properties,

36
00:02:06,393 --> 00:02:08,464
the environment and the greeting.

37
00:02:08,464 --> 00:02:10,033
And a profile named prod,

38
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:11,916
which changes the environment.

39
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:17,031
The file that uses them. Type: cat src/main/resources/app.properties

40
00:02:17,161 --> 00:02:20,959
Two dollar names. Maven will fill them in when it copies the file.

41
00:02:21,509 --> 00:02:28,186
Build, then run. Type: mvn -B -q package && java -jar target/shop-1.0.0.jar

42
00:02:28,316 --> 00:02:31,281
Quiet mode, so only errors print. A few seconds.

43
00:02:37,831 --> 00:02:43,101
Dev. The jar ran, because the plug in set the main class. And both names were filled in.

44
00:02:43,651 --> 00:02:45,477
Now the prod profile.

45
00:02:45,477 --> 00:02:51,651
Type: mvn -B -q clean package -Pprod && java -jar target/shop-1.0.0.jar

46
00:02:51,781 --> 00:02:53,615
Same code, different profile.

47
00:03:01,165 --> 00:03:03,171
Prod. One switch changed the build.

48
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:06,057
And the command line wins.

49
00:03:06,057 --> 00:03:13,427
Type: mvn -B -q clean package -Dapp.greeting=Hi && java -jar target/shop-1.0.0.jar

50
00:03:13,557 --> 00:03:14,603
One more build.

51
00:03:22,153 --> 00:03:25,289
The greeting is now Hi. The command line beat the pom.

52
00:03:25,839 --> 00:03:27,490
Check the plug in's work.

53
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:32,047
Type: unzip -p target/shop-1.0.0.jar META-INF/MANIFEST.MF | grep Main

54
00:03:32,177 --> 00:03:35,078
There is the main class, written by the jar plug in.

55
00:03:36,628 --> 00:03:39,785
Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

56
00:03:40,335 --> 00:03:40,822
Build,

57
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:43,578
and take a fingerprint of the jar.

58
00:03:43,578 --> 00:03:49,658
Type: mvn -B -q clean package; sha256sum target/shop-1.0.0.jar | cut -c1-16

59
00:03:49,788 --> 00:03:53,671
A fingerprint, called a hash, changes if even one byte changes.

60
00:03:58,221 --> 00:04:00,162
Remember the start of that fingerprint.

61
00:04:00,712 --> 00:04:00,870
Again,

62
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:01,107
the same.

63
00:04:01,107 --> 00:04:03,080
Type: mvn -B -q clean package; sha256sum target/shop-1.0.0.jar | cut -c1-16

64
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:08,746
Nothing in the code changed.

65
00:04:14,296 --> 00:04:19,011
A different fingerprint. The jar stores the build time, so every build is different.

66
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:19,948
Fix it.

67
00:04:19,948 --> 00:04:27,411
Type: sed -i '8a\    <project.build.outputTimestamp>2024-01-01T00:00:00Z</project.build.outputTimestamp>' pom.xml; sed -n 8,10p pom.xml

68
00:04:30,741 --> 00:04:36,864
One property, project dot build dot output time stamp. A fixed date, stored in every file.

69
00:04:37,414 --> 00:04:37,497
Build,

70
00:04:37,497 --> 00:04:37,719
and fingerprint.

71
00:04:37,719 --> 00:04:38,758
Type: mvn -B -q clean package; sha256sum target/shop-1.0.0.jar | cut -c1-16

72
00:04:43,888 --> 00:04:44,763
Once more.

73
00:04:50,313 --> 00:04:52,105
Note the start of this fingerprint.

74
00:04:52,655 --> 00:04:52,791
Again,

75
00:04:52,791 --> 00:04:52,996
the same.

76
00:04:52,996 --> 00:04:54,703
Type: mvn -B -q clean package; sha256sum target/shop-1.0.0.jar | cut -c1-16

77
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:00,157
The same again.

78
00:05:05,707 --> 00:05:11,040
The same fingerprint, twice. A reproducible build. Same input, same bytes, anywhere.

79
00:05:12,590 --> 00:05:13,507
Your lab task.

80
00:05:13,507 --> 00:05:16,718
Lab eighty-seven gives you a jar that cannot run,

81
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:18,160
with settings not set.

82
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:19,929
Lab check judges your work.

83
00:05:20,479 --> 00:05:22,631
It lives at git hub dot com,

84
00:05:22,631 --> 00:05:24,169
slash it dash waves,

85
00:05:24,169 --> 00:05:25,937
slash dev ops dash A I,

86
00:05:25,937 --> 00:05:26,783
in academy,

87
00:05:26,783 --> 00:05:27,090
lab,

88
00:05:27,090 --> 00:05:29,012
lab eighty-seven dot S H.

89
00:05:29,562 --> 00:05:34,338
One: configure the jar plug in so the main class is cloud cart dot hello,

90
00:05:34,338 --> 00:05:36,431
and java dash jar prints a line.

91
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:42,016
Two: add the two properties, and turn on filtering. The jar must print dev, welcome.

92
00:05:42,566 --> 00:05:46,683
Three: add a profile called prod, that sets the environment to prod.

93
00:05:47,233 --> 00:05:52,183
Four: add the output time stamp property, so two builds make an identical jar.

94
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:56,189
Five: build, and save the fingerprint into shaw dot text.

95
00:05:56,739 --> 00:05:59,063
Install a J D K and Maven first.

96
00:05:59,063 --> 00:05:59,935
Then lab up,

97
00:05:59,935 --> 00:06:00,662
lab check,

98
00:06:00,662 --> 00:06:01,606
and lab down.

99
00:06:01,606 --> 00:06:04,803
The shortcut file is shortcuts eighty-seven.

100
00:06:06,853 --> 00:06:08,922
Pass or fail. You need all six.

101
00:06:09,472 --> 00:06:11,286
Five pass lines in lab check.

102
00:06:11,836 --> 00:06:14,822
You can say what a plug in configuration block does.

103
00:06:15,372 --> 00:06:18,039
You can explain why the command line beats the pom.

104
00:06:18,589 --> 00:06:20,168
You can switch a profile on.

105
00:06:20,718 --> 00:06:23,278
You can say why two builds differ, and the fix.

106
00:06:23,828 --> 00:06:25,748
lab down, nothing left behind.

107
00:06:26,298 --> 00:06:27,599
To pass, all six.

108
00:06:29,649 --> 00:06:32,486
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

109
00:06:33,036 --> 00:06:35,127
One: lab up, and read the task file.

110
00:06:35,677 --> 00:06:38,088
Two: fix the manifest, and run the jar.

111
00:06:38,638 --> 00:06:40,771
Three: add the properties and filtering.

112
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:44,073
Four: add the prod profile, and the time stamp.

113
00:06:44,623 --> 00:06:47,354
Five: build, save the fingerprint, and lab check.

114
00:06:47,904 --> 00:06:50,485
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

115
